<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · conquest</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conquest of Mecca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/629">629</a>–<a href="/years/630">630</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Fath Makka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> was a campaign by Muhammad and his companions against the Quraysh-controlled city in December 629 or January 630 (10–20 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>, 8 AH); sources vary on the dates. It followed an attack by the Banu Bakr, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> support, on the Banu Khuza'ah, allies of the Muslims, which breached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hudaybiyyah">Treaty of Hudaybiyyah</a>. Abu Sufyan ibn Harb sought a renewed truce in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> without success. Muhammad entered the city, proclaimed a general amnesty with a few exceptions, and cleared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> of its idols, which tradition numbers at 360. The fall of Mecca formally ended the conflict between the Muslims and the Quraysh.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 628 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and the Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> made a ten-year pact, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hudaybiyyah">Treaty of Hudaybiyyah</a>. The Banu Bakr aligned with the Quraysh and the Banu Khuza'ah with the Muslims. Old grievances from the pre-Islamic era, sharpened by a desire for revenge, led to renewed fighting, and the treaty was breached when the Banu Bakr attacked the Banu Khuza'ah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>According to Al-Tabari, three days before the news of the breach arrived, Muhammad told his wife '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a> to prepare for a campaign. 'Amr ibn Salim Al-Khuza'i then reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> with forty horsemen to seek help for his people. Muhammad summoned all Muslims able to fight and set out, joined on the way by his uncle Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib. Accounts differ on the dates: he is variously said to have left on 2, 6 or 10 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> and to have entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> on 10, 17 or 18, 19 or 20 Ramadan 8 AH.</p><p>Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, with Hakim ibn Hizam and Budayl ibn Warqa', went out to learn the Muslims' position and met Abbas, who brought them to Muhammad. Abu Sufyan accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Muhammad declared that whoever took refuge in Abu Sufyan's house, stayed in his own house or entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram">Sacred Mosque</a> would be safe. Muhammad then divided his army: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> led the right flank into the lower part of the city, Zubayr ibn al-Awwam the left from above, and Abu 'Ubaidah the infantry through a side valley, with orders to kill only in self-defence. Khalid met resistance led by Ikrima ibn Amr, Safwan ibn Umayya and Suhayl ibn Amr; twelve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> and two of his own men were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Muhammad went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>, took the key from 'Uthman ibn Talha and ordered the idols and images inside destroyed. He then addressed the Meccans from the doorway, and Bilal ibn Rabah called the adhan from the Kaaba. Muhammad had decreed that nine people might be killed even under the curtains of the Kaaba; four were put to death, including 'Abdullah ibn Khatal and Miqyas ibn Sababa, who had killed Muslims and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Others, among them Ikrima, Wahshi and Hind bint Utba, were forgiven.</p><p>On the second day in the city Muhammad declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> a sanctuary. Muhammad stayed nineteen days, teaching, sending envoys to invite people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and sending men to destroy remaining idols near Mecca, among them al-Uzza at Nakhlah and Suwa' of the Hudhayl.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conquest of Mecca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muawiyah belonged to the Umayyad clan of the Quraysh, the clan that had led Meccan opposition to Muhammad until the Prophet's <b>conquest of Mecca</b> in 630.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Abdullah ibn Muhammad Aal Ash-Shaykh condemned military excesses during the <b>conquest of Mecca</b>, saying Arabs would not be taken captive and that killing women and children was not agreed to.</blockquote><a href="/movement/wahhabism">Wahhabism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
